{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness, specifically defining a lottery as right-skewed when a greater portion of probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes (i.e., the low outcome, 0 Euros) compared to favorable outcomes (i.e., the high outcome, 2500 or 2400 Euros). Skewness in this context is about the distribution of probabilities: if more probability is on the 0 Euro outcome (unfavorable), then the lottery is right-skewed. The reason prefers the more right-skewed lottery. In this problem, both lotteries have a non-zero probability of yielding 0 Euros: Lottery A has 66% chance of 0 Euros, Lottery B has 67% chance of 0 Euros. Therefore, both lotteries are right-skewed, but B has a slightly higher probability of the unfavorable outcome (0 Euros). Thus, B is more right-skewed than A. The decision reason would therefore prefer Lottery B. The relevant information for the reason is the probability mass on the 0 Euro outcome (unfavorable) and the high outcome (favorable). This information is fully present in the lotteries: A has 66% on 0, B has 67% on 0. Hence, the decision reason can be applied to the decision problem.",
  "step_2": "The verbal report is: 'Mehr Geld für quasi die selbe Wahrscheinlichkeit' which translates to 'More money for almost the same probability.' This indicates that the individual is focusing on the monetary amount (higher payout) and the similarity in probabilities. The individual is not discussing probabilities of 0 Euros, nor is there any mention of skewness, distribution, or unfavorable outcomes. The deliberation centers on the amount of money (2500 vs. 2400) and the near-identical probabilities (33% vs. 34% and 67% vs. 66%). There is no reference to the structure of the probability distribution, the likelihood of losing, or the concept of skewness. The justification is based on the value of the payout and the similarity of probabilities, not on the distributional shape or skewness of the outcomes. Therefore, the individual's reasoning is based on expected value or utility of the monetary gain, not on skewness.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason — specifically, the probability of the unfavorable outcome (0 Euros) — is not considered by the individual. The individual does not mention the 67% vs. 66% probability of 0 Euros or the distributional shape of the lotteries. Instead, the individual focuses on the higher monetary amount (2500 vs. 2400) and the similarity of probabilities. The decision reason's rule — preferring the more right-skewed lottery — is not reflected in the justification. Since the individual did not consider the skewness or the distributional imbalance, and instead focused on the magnitude of the payout, the decision reason was not used. Therefore, the individual did not use the skewness-based reason in making the decision.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

