{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness of lotteries, specifically defining a lottery as right-skewed when a greater portion of probability is assigned to unfavorable (negative) outcomes compared to favorable (positive) ones. In this case, both lotteries have a negative outcome (loss) with a certain probability and a zero outcome (no loss) with the remaining probability. The key is to assess whether the distribution of losses (i.e., the probability of losing more money) is greater in one lottery than the other. Lottery A has a 20% chance of losing 4000 Euros and an 80% chance of losing nothing. Lottery B has a 25% chance of losing 3000 Euros and a 75% chance of losing nothing. Since both lotteries have a negative outcome only in the first case, and the magnitude of the loss is higher in A (4000 vs. 3000), we must assess the skewness. A lottery is right-skewed if more probability mass is on the unfavorable side. However, in both cases, the unfavorable outcome occurs with a small probability (20% vs. 25%), and the rest of the probability is on the zero outcome. Thus, the probability of a loss is higher in B (25%) than in A (20%), and the loss in A is larger. The decision reason prefers the more right-skewed lottery — meaning the one with a greater probability of a large loss. Since B has a higher probability of a loss (25% > 20%), and the loss is smaller (3000 < 4000), the skewness is not clearly more on the unfavorable side in either. In fact, the distribution of losses is more concentrated in B (smaller loss, higher probability), which may suggest left skewness. Thus, A has a larger loss with lower probability, which could be seen as more right-skewed (tail on the negative side). However, the decision reason defines right-skewness as greater probability on unfavorable outcomes. Since both have small unfavorable probabilities, and B has a slightly higher unfavorable probability (25% > 20%), B is more skewed toward the unfavorable side. Therefore, the reason would prefer B. The information relevant to the decision reason is thus: A has 20% chance of -4000, B has 25% chance of -3000. The reason prefers the more right-skewed lottery — i.e., the one with a greater probability of a negative outcome. Thus, B has a higher probability of a loss (25% > 20%), so it is more right-skewed. The reason would thus prefer B.",
  "step_2": "The verbal report states: 'Ähnlich wie gerade eben, eine ähnliche Chance, Geld zu verlieren, aber minimal höhere Chance, mehr weniger Geld zu verlieren. Das ist okay.' This translates to: 'Similar to just now, a similar chance of losing money, but slightly higher chance of losing more money. That's okay.' The individual notes that the chance of losing money is similar, but slightly higher in one lottery (implied to be A, since it has a larger loss), and that this is acceptable. The individual is not focusing on the probability of loss but rather on the magnitude of loss. The individual describes a slight increase in the chance of losing more money (i.e., a larger loss), which suggests a concern about the severity of loss, not about the skewness of the distribution. The individual does not mention probability distribution, skewness, or any comparison of the probability of unfavorable outcomes. The justification centers on the idea that the loss is slightly worse but still acceptable — not on whether one lottery is more right-skewed. Thus, the individual does not refer to skewness or the distribution of probabilities across outcomes, nor does the individual compare the probability of unfavorable outcomes. The deliberation process is based on risk tolerance and acceptability of larger losses, not on skewness.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires comparing the skewness of the lotteries, specifically the probability mass on unfavorable outcomes. The individual does not mention skewness, distribution, or the probability of unfavorable outcomes in their reasoning. Instead, the individual focuses on the magnitude of the loss (losing more money) and the slight increase in the chance of such a loss. This reflects a concern about loss severity, not distributional skewness. The individual does not evaluate whether one lottery has a greater probability of a negative outcome, nor do they refer to the concept of right-skewness. Therefore, the decision reason — which depends on comparing the probability of unfavorable outcomes — is not used in the verbal report. The individual’s justification is based on a different reasoning process (risk acceptance), not on skewness. Thus, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

